'''Shinrikyō''' (神理教) is a sect of {{ill|Sect Shinto|ja|教派神道|12=draft|12=draft}} founded by {{ill|Sano Tsunehiko|ja|佐野経彦|lt=Kannagibe Tsunehiko|lt_ja=巫部経彦|12=draft|12=draft}} based on the traditional ''Kannagibe Shintō'' (巫部神道). Primarily active around {{ill|Kokura|ja|小倉|12=draft|12=draft}} in {{ill|Fukuoka Prefecture|ja|福岡県|12=draft|12=draft}} on {{ill|Kyushu|ja|九州|12=draft|12=draft}}, Shinrikyō disseminated teachings of {{ill|Koshintō|ja|古神道|12=draft|12=draft}}.
== Overview == Shinrikyō traces its lineage to {{ill|Nigihayahi|ja|饒速日命|12=draft|12=draft}} through the Kannagibe genealogy{{sfn|井上順孝|1991|p=226}}{{sfn|井上順孝|1991|pp=150-151}}. Founded officially in 1884 after receiving initial permission as Shinrikyōkai from the Shintō Secretariat in 1880, it became independent from {{ill|Ontake-kyō|ja|御嶽教|12=draft|12=draft}} in 1894. Since June 14, 1952, it has been registered as a religious corporation under Japanese law{{sfn|井上順孝ほか編|1996|p=134}}.
It venerates eighteen heavenly deities, classified as ''Amenimasu Moromoro no Kami'' (天在諸神), from {{ill|Amenominakanushi|ja|天之御中主神|12=draft|12=draft}} to {{ill|Amaterasu Ōmikami|ja|天照皇太神|12=draft|12=draft}}. According to Japan's Agency for Cultural Affairs, Shinrikyō belongs to the {{ill|Restoration Shintō|ja|復古神道|12=draft|12=draft}} category within Sect Shintō{{sfn|文化庁編さん|2011|pp=5-6}}.
== Kannagibe Shintō == Sano inherited family records tracing back to Nigihayahi, becoming the 77th generation{{sfn|井上順孝|1991|pp=150-155}}. He developed Shinrikyō based on these traditions.
===Kannagibe lineage=== 1.{{ill|Nigihayahi|ja|饒速日命|12=draft|12=draft}}, 2.{{ill|Umashimaji|ja|宇麻志麻知命|12=draft|12=draft}}, (continued lineage up to) 77. Sano Tsunehiko{{sfn|井上順孝|1991|pp=150-151}}.
== Founding of Shinrikyōkai == In 1877, Sano began preaching publicly and founded Shinrikyōkai officially in 1880. After gaining recognition from imperial princes and unsuccessful attempts to acquire sacred relics from Ise Shrine, it became independent in 1894.
== Teachings and Features == The primary doctrine appears in the "Shinrizu" (神理図), focusing on cosmology, ethics, and divine influence on human life{{sfn|井上順孝|1991|pp=214-220}}.
===Deities=== Primary eighteen gods include Amenominakanushi, Takamimusubi, Kamimusubi, and Amaterasu. Others revered include Tsukuyomi and Susanoo{{sfn|井上順孝|1991|p=229}}.
===Organization and Expansion=== Headquarters remain in Kitakyushu. At its peak in 1906, it claimed 1.5 million adherents. Today it has 31,945 followers{{sfn|文化庁『宗教年鑑 令和6年版』|p=61}}.
== Leadership == * First leader: Sano Tsunehiko * Current (fifth) leader: Kannagibe Sukehiko (巫部祐彦)
== Headquarters == Tokuriki 5-10-8, Kokuraminami-ku, Kitakyushu, Fukuoka Prefecture
== Access == * Nishitetsu Bus Kitakyushu, Shinrikyō-mae stop * Kitakyushu Monorail, Tokuriki Arashiyamaguchi Station
== Publications == Monthly journal: "Shinri," established in 1912.
